{
  "topic": "Types of Fractions",
  "affirmation": "You taught fractions with warm, concrete examples and clear voice.",
  "identity": "You teach by bringing maths into students' everyday world: you moved from a drawn circle and rectangles to a cake story and birthday example so children could picture 'parts of a whole.' You keep the class talking and counting aloud, asking short, direct prompts that let many students join in.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Student raw reasoning",
      "quote": "10 میں سے 9، 9، 10 میں سے 1 گئے تو 9 بچ گیا۔ 9 اور 10 نیچے، اور 9 اوپر۔",
      "why": "This moment shows you created space for students to put ideas into words, revealing exactly where their notation and language were tangled."
    },
    {
      "title": "Everyday anchor",
      "quote": "مثلاً آپ اس کو سمجھیں یہ کیک ہے۔ کیک پسند ہے نا سب کو؟",
      "why": "Using cake made the idea of equal parts instantly relatable and kept the class engaged in the 'why' behind partitioning."
    },
    {
      "title": "Student concept phrasing",
      "quote": "جو کلر کیا ہوا ہے، وہ اوپر ہے، اور ٹوٹل نیچے ہے۔",
      "why": "This student phrasing distilled the core fraction idea into a simple sentence, signaling emerging understanding you had been steering toward."
    }
  ],
  "strength_name": "Concrete visual models",
  "strength_note": "You repeatedly used circles, rectangles, colored parts and a cake example to anchor numerator/denominator language and counting.",
  "horizon_title": "One scaffold and extension",
  "horizon_note": "Add one small, easier prompt or template for students who struggle and one short, harder task for early finishers so learners can work at different levels.",
  "journey_note": "This single lesson peaked at 57% on observed indicators, showing a clear, teachable pattern of strong visuals and whole-class checks that stop short of deeper student reasoning.",
  "score_framing": "57% is a mid-journey snapshot: you launched with warmth and strong concrete models, and a few targeted shifts will deepen learning.",
  "domain_whys": {
    "lesson_plan_fidelity": "This is developing because you used the planned visual models and moved from whole to parts when you partitioned the circle and the rectangle — it's not full marks because you did not write and define numerator and denominator clearly on the board.",
    "high_leverage_practices": "This is developing because you invited students to count and repeat labels aloud (for example asking 'اوپر والے نمبر کو کیا کہتے ہیں؟' and having them say 'نمبریٹر') — it's not full marks because you did not offer students choices of method or encourage student-led explanations beyond short responses.",
    "student_engagement": "This is strong because students repeatedly answered, counted partitions aloud, and connected learning to the cake example and personal stories — it's not full marks because most replies were short or procedural and no content-focused student questions emerged.",
    "teacher_subject_knowledge": "This is developing because you correctly named numerator and denominator and corrected placement (for example '9 ہم نے اوپر لکھنا ہے، اور 10؟') — it's not full marks because you did not present a multi-step challenge or allow sustained wait time to surface common misconceptions like mixing total parts with shaded parts."
  }
}
